{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context and confirmed root cause\n\nTwo accepted reliability oracles still consume multi-second wall time per matrix cell:\n\n- `test_slow_but_progressing_remote_is_not_aborted` serves a real loopback remote for 15 seconds (15.14s in the current macOS-shaped run). The contract is that sustained bytes above the configured floor survive one complete low-speed window; production's ten-second window is not itself test behavior.\n- `test_read_retried_then_terminal` uses a one-second call timeout plus production backoff sleeps across three real child processes (~9s). The contract is exactly three attempts, the logical 2s/4s retry schedule, terminal timeout, and no surviving process group—not nine elapsed seconds.\n\nProduction defaults must remain unchanged.\n\n## OSS consensus\n\nApproved. CPython's subprocess suite uses 0.05–0.1s test trigger timeouts with generous cleanup polling/deadlines, and urllib3 uses millisecond-scale test-only network timeouts while keeping real sockets. References and the cross-ticket rejection check are recorded in the existing OSS-consensus comment. Neither suave-constant-cow nor fizzy-trunk-salt requires production-scale seconds in tests.\n\n## Plan\n\n- In `tests/unit/test_snapshot_fetch_stall_abort_12e4.py`, inject a one-second low-speed window into the slow-progress test, keep the real loopback socket and above-floor byte pacing active for more than one full configured window, and retain the semantic assertion that the failure is not a stall.\n- In `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/state/test_acli_subprocess_timeout.py`, inject sub-second call/grace/drain values through the existing function/constant seams; replace real retry sleeps with a recorder that asserts the exact 2s/4s logical schedule.\n- Strengthen the read-retry sentinel so each real spawned PID is recorded and polled after terminal failure, proving no child/process group survives. Keep the production retry count and real subprocess execution.\n- Use generous cleanup polling only; add no brittle wall-clock pass threshold and change no production code/default.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] Slow-progress uses a real loopback socket, transfers above the configured floor for more than one complete injected low-speed window, and is not classified as a stall.\n- [ ] ACLI read retry launches exactly `_MAX_ATTEMPTS` real process sessions and observes the exact production retry schedule `[2, 4]` without sleeping it.\n- [ ] Every PID launched by the ACLI read sentinel is absent after terminal failure, using polling with a generous cleanup deadline.\n- [ ] Production timeout, stall, grace, drain, attempt, and backoff defaults are byte-unchanged.\n- [ ] Both focused modules pass and their two target tests complete in low single-digit seconds without an absolute upper-bound assertion.\n\n## File impact\n\n- `tests/unit/test_snapshot_fetch_stall_abort_12e4.py`\n- `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/state/test_acli_subprocess_timeout.py`\n"}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786645754582487001,"uuid":"3674047e-cfd1-4c5e-b229-ef3f5213c110"}